-- title:
--   Integral ratios in the q-expansion field lie in intFormRatiosC
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be a field and let $\Gamma$ be a subgroup of $\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$ containing the translation matrix $\mathrm{ModularGroup.T}$. Write $\mathrm{intSeriesC}\,K\,p$ for the Laurent series over $K$ obtained from a power series $p \in \mathbb{Z}[[q]]$ by reducing its coefficients along $\mathbb{Z} \to K$ and viewing the result in $K((q))$, and let $\mathrm{intFormRatiosC}\,K\,\Gamma \subseteq K((q))$ be the set of Laurent series of the form $\mathrm{intSeriesC}\,K\,p_f / \mathrm{intSeriesC}\,K\,p_g$ where, for one common weight $k \in \mathbb{Z}$, $f$ and $g$ are modular forms of weight $k$ for the image of $\Gamma$ in $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{R})$ whose width-one $q$-expansions at $\infty$ are the images of integral power series $p_f, p_g \in \mathbb{Z}[[q]]$ under $\mathbb{Z} \to \mathbb{C}$, and $\mathrm{intSeriesC}\,K\,p_g \neq 0$. Let $x$ be an element of the intermediate field $K(\mathrm{intFormRatiosC}\,K\,\Gamma)$ of $K((q))$, and suppose that there are $P, Q \in \mathbb{Z}[[q]]$ with $\mathrm{intSeriesC}\,K\,Q \neq 0$ and $x = \mathrm{intSeriesC}\,K\,P / \mathrm{intSeriesC}\,K\,Q$ as Laurent series. The conclusion is that $x$ itself belongs to $\mathrm{intFormRatiosC}\,K\,\Gamma$, i.e. is a single ratio of reductions of integral $q$-expansions of two modular forms of equal weight for $\Gamma$.
--
--   This is a saturation statement for the generating set of the $q$-expansion function field: an element of the field generated by ratios of integral modular $q$-expansions which is visibly a ratio of reductions of integral power series is already such a ratio, with no adjunction of $K$-coefficients needed. It is used in the comparison of diamond operators on the mod-$\ell$ $q$-expansion function field, via [`ModularCurve.IsDiamondPullbackModL.coe_apply_eq_of_mem_Gamma0_of_level_mul`](thm.html#ModularCurve.IsDiamondPullbackModL.coe_apply_eq_of_mem_Gamma0_of_level_mul).
